# Phase 005 Group 05 — high-consequence authority

Status: **Accepted — AUTH-033–AUTH-043. AUTH-001–AUTH-043 accepted overall. Group 06 is next and not started.**

Preserve:

- proposal/request ≠ approval/authorization ≠ action issuance ≠ control-plane acceptance ≠ enforcement/effect ≠ outcome;
- high-consequence-use eligibility ≠ control capability;
- confirmation authority ≠ confirmation evidence sufficiency;
- job operation ≠ raw-data access ≠ gate authority ≠ safeguard authority;
- gate configuration ≠ normal hold/admit operation ≠ override ≠ enforcement;
- safeguard proposal ≠ activation ≠ release ≠ health;
- capability exercise ≠ delegation authority;
- delegated grant ≠ implicit transitive re-delegation;
- break-glass ≠ universal superuser ≠ evidence/readiness/health/cause truth;
- service/model technical ability ≠ high-consequence authority;
- authorization unavailable/conflicting/unknown ≠ invented allow/deny;
- existing control state ≠ authority to change that state;
- current revocation ≠ rewritten historical authorization/action.

Rules:

- Bind every high-consequence authorization to exact principal, action stage/class, target, environment/context, time, and applicable profile/rule.
- Causal `confirmed` requires both REF-013–REF-020 and an explicitly authorized confirmer for the claim class/profile.
- Human/automated confirmation is profile-specific; automation can confirm only where explicitly permitted.
- Retry/restart/cancel/trigger and other job actions are granular capabilities and do not imply data access or success.
- Gate registration/configuration/enable/disable/normal operation/override/retire may have separate principals.
- Override never converts prerequisite state into ready.
- Safeguard release is independently high consequence because it restores propagation; release never proves health.
- Multi-party/quorum/separation/self-approval requirements exist only when explicit; duplicate approval evidence cannot satisfy distinct-approval requirements.
- Delegation requires explicit grant authority, scope, time, and re-delegation semantics.
- Break-glass requires explicit governing basis, qualifying emergency condition, bounded action/scope/time, provenance, and any review requirements.
- Authorization-source failure does not itself trigger break-glass or fallback authority.
- Service principals need exact action capability; code deployment, API reachability, scheduler ownership, or model recommendation is not permission.
- Fallback on authorization outage is action-specific; never invent global fail-open/fail-closed/hold/release behavior.
- Authorization/approval never proves action delivery, enforcement, successful execution, healthy data, prevented exposure, or causal correctness.

Do not select IAM/RBAC/ABAC, approval engine, scheduler/orchestrator, quarantine/control implementation, causal engine, or automated-confirmation mechanism in Phase 005.
